when it comes to PC gaming the monitor  you choose matters a whole lot more than  people think sure you can buy any basic  1080p monitor for around $100 but a  gaming monitor is built for gaming with  gaming specific features so in this  video we're comparing four of the top  gaming monitors under $200 to see which  one you should buy for your gaming needs  and we'll have link to these monitors in  the video description below the first  one is a 24 inch curved monitor from  sceptre for a hundred and sixty three  dollars than the 24 inch Zowie monitor  from BenQ for $199 than the 24 inch Acer  nitro monitor for a hundred and sixty  dollars and finally a 27 inch monitor  from Dell for a hundred and eighty  dollars which we've already covered in a  previous video for being the  best-selling gaming monitor on Amazon  before we get into build quality and  design let's compare some specs the  resolutions the same across the board  with full HD 1080p and for panel tech  the Dell and Ben Q get a TN panel while  the sceptre gets VA and the Acer gets  IPs the difference is seen in a few  different things mainly including  response time viewing angles color  accuracy price and refresh rates for  viewing angles the Acer and it's IPS  panel easily beats out the rest and it's  the same thing for color accuracy as  well  however IPS panels are more costly to  produce especially with high refresh  rates and that's why the Acer only  refreshes at 75 Hertz compared to 144  Hertz on the rest of these panels the  144 Hertz model of the IPS monitor costs  a much higher 285 dollars in IPS panels  typically have slower response times but  with a sirs visual response boost mode  on it'll hit a one millisecond response  time but some users complain about  flickering issues and monitor brightness  going down with VRB mode enabled which  may be an issue since it's already rated  at only 250 nits of brightness with the  dell being rated up to 400 nits with a  sir's visual response boost disabled  response time goes up to 10 milliseconds  a lot slower than the rest  the Behnke monitor is the only monitor  out of the bunch that doesn't support  free sync a technology that helps get  rid of screen tearing and stuttering  when FPS doesn't match them on  refresh rate they all have a cements but  the scepter has a smaller 75 by 75  millimeter pattern so be aware of that  now let's compare the physical design of  the monitors from the front that Acer  looks the best with the thinnest bezels  overall and the best looking stand out  of the bunch  the BenQ has huge bezels all around in  an enlarged area for the controls not to  mention a massive and thick stand so  it's not the best choice for looks from  the side the scepter is very thin and  sleek compared to the rest and it's the  only one that's curved the BenQ and Acer  are by far the thickest from the side  due to the scepters low-profile stand  the display sits closer to the wall than  the rest of the monitors with Adel in  second place and been cues display  sitting the furthest from the wall so if  you have a small desk  the scepter ones here as for the stands  the scepters is the only one that's made  out of a solid piece of aluminum  compared to plastic stands with aluminum  plates and the scepter is by far the  sturdiest when you wiggle the desk with  a larger BenQ in dell wobbling the most  as for adjustability the BenQ destroys  the rest of the monitors would tilt side  to side swivel up and down height in  ninety degree rotation adjustability for  a vertical set up the rest of the  monitors literally only tilt forward and  backward the main drawback is the lack  of height adjustments since some of  these monitors sit pretty low as for  cable management the Dell and Acer have  basically none at all the sceptre comes  with a couple of plastic Clips that you  can adjust and they work very well and  the BenQ has a plastic clip on the back  which works great as well as for the  cables that come in the box  the sceptre is the only one that comes  with an external power brick the rest of  them have power supplies built into the  monitor however the sceptre does come  with the DisplayPort cable in same with  the BenQ monitor as well the Dell comes  with an HDMI 2.0 cable which isn't as  good as having DisplayPort since Nvidia  graphics cards require display ports as  a port AMD freesync now the Acer  actually came with an ancient VGA cable  which is so old that the budget rx 580  from 2017  doesn't support it so you will have to  buy an HDMI cable to reach 75 Hertz  since the monitor also doesn't support  DisplayPort now onto the i/o the Acer is  limited to two HDMI 1.4  a VGA port an audio import for using  your monitor speakers when you're using  VGA and a headphone jack the sceptre  comes with two clearly labeled HDMI  ports a display port also capable of 144  Hertz and a headphone jack the dell has  hdmi and displayport both capable of 144  Hertz a headphone jack in two USB 2.0  ports for peripherals the BenQ comes  with the DisplayPort and dual link DVI  port both capable of 144 Hertz and an  HDMI port which I believe is limited to  60 Hertz it also has a headphone jack on  the left side which may be more  comfortable to use and it's the only one  without built-in speakers which honestly  doesn't really matter now firing them up  let's compare the menu controls and UI  the Dell has clearly labeled controls on  the front that are very easy to use and  the UI is very simple and works  perfectly the sceptre has three buttons  on the back which you'll have to take a  look at to understand and it'll take  some time to get used to the UI is a bit  cluttered but it works ok overall the  Acer has a joystick that can be used to  change every setting and it works really  well along with three more buttons used  to quickly change things like input and  brightness the UI is simple and looks  better than Dells however when you turn  on one millisecond response time VRB  mode the screen dims like crazy and  freesync actually turns off so you can't  get both at the same time you'll be  stuck at a horrible 10 milliseconds  response time if you want free sync and  a brighter display onto the BenQ it has  an unnecessary bulge on the right side  for its unlabeled controls which don't  work very well because the UI was a pain  to use compared to the rest of the  monitors and finally we did some gaming  playing fortnight on each monitor to  test out how well each one performed the  sceptre played very well at 144 Hertz  using free sync and we didn't notice any  issues with gameplay except that the  display was a bit dim the dáil also  played great at 144 Hertz and there were  no issues and I loved how it was super  bright and the colors look pretty good  as well  the BenQ however seemed to play at 144  Hertz pretty well despite not having  free sync with great brightness and  decent colors too now the Acer was  another story it's limited to 75 Hertz  and since the 1 millisecond virtual  response boost mode dims the brightness  as flickering and  was freesync I turned it off and dealt  with ten millisecond response time so it  didn't feel that smooth compared to the  rest because of that and the fact that  it comes with the VGA cable the Acer is  out leaving the other three the BenQ was  in third place for having the huge  bezels being very bulky overall with bad  controls and the worst UI along with the  lack of free sync we did enjoy all the  adjustability and good brightness but  it's the most expensive at $199 the  sceptre just barely got into second  place by being priced at a hundred and  sixty dollars the 144 Hertz support with  either HDMI or display part was  excellent in the low profile design with  the sturdiest and helped outweigh the  negatives of the dimmer display and the  subpar menu controls and the winner is  the dell for quite a few reasons  you get 144 Hertz with either HDMI or  DisplayPort and it's the only one that  packs two USB ports you get free sync a  low two millisecond response time the  brightest display of all of these and  the largest at 27 inches with pretty  slim bezels as well the controls are  simple and intuitive and fortnight  played great at 144 Hertz the only  downsides are the slightly less sturdy  stand in the lack of cable management  other than that there's really no  compromises at a great price of a  hundred and eighty dollars and that's  why it's the winner if you want to learn  more about that display check out a  review of the dell after this video  we've left links of the best deals on  all these monitors in the description  below and if you enjoyed this video and  you've made your decision  use those links below to help support us  and click that button above to subscribe  this has been of a team with max TAC  thanks for watching and we'll see you in  the next video
 

